==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|totheles}}, {{m|enm|toþeles}}, from {{inh|en|ang|tōþlēas}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*tanþlausaz|t=toothless}}, equivalent to {{suffix|en|tooth|less}}. Cognate with {{cog|nl|tandeloos|t=toothless}}, {{cog|nds-de|tannlos|t=toothless}}, {{cog|de|zahnlos|t=toothless}}, {{cog|da|tandløs|t=toothless}}, {{cog|sv|tandlös|t=toothless}}, {{cog|is|tannlaus|t=toothless}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|GA}} {{IPA|en|/ˈtuθləs/|/ˈtuθlɪs/}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Having no [[teeth]].
#: {{ux|en|a '''toothless''' old man}}
# {{lb|en|figuratively}} [[weak|Weak]]; having no ability to enforce something.
#: {{ux|en|The treaty was '''toothless''' because of the lack of participation from the undersigned.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1603|author=w:Ben Jonson|title=w:The Entertainment at Althorp
|passage=Not tell? ha! ha! I could smile / At this old and '''toothless''' wile.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2021-10-10|author=Caroline Anders|title=A TikTok bone salesman’s wall of spines reignites ethical debate over selling human remains|journal=w:The Washington Post|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/science/2021/10/09/tiktok-jonsbones-human-remains-wall-spines/|passage=Bans of the sale of human remains across platforms like Facebook, Etsy, Instagram and eBay are '''toothless''' and poorly enforced, Huffer said. And when a page does get shut down, he said, it just pops up on a different website.}}

